A small yellow dot near the tip of a nail is the entire reason this condition spreads as often as it does. There is no pain. There is nothing to suggest urgency. So it gets ignored, filed down, painted over, and revisited weeks later when it has already doubled in size. That delay is the single biggest factor in how far <strong>nail fungus symptoms</strong> are allowed to travel before anyone does anything about them. <strong>Nail fungus symptoms</strong> rarely cause pain in the early stage, which is exactly why infections go unnoticed for so long. This guide walks through what <strong>nail fungus symptoms</strong> actually look like at every stage, from the first faint spot to the signs that mean a nail has been infected for months. Recognising <strong>nail fungus symptoms</strong> early is the difference between a quick fix and a months-long recovery. If you are still unclear on <a href='/blog/nail-fungus/what-is-nail-fungus'>what nail fungus is</a>, that guide covers the basics in more depth. Here, the focus stays narrow and practical: comparing what is on your nail right now to what each stage of <strong>nail fungus symptoms</strong> actually looks like, so you know whether to monitor it, treat it, or book an appointment.

