## What Nail Fungus Symptoms Look Like in the Early Stage

The first signs of <strong>nail fungus symptoms</strong> are typically a small white or yellow spot beneath the tip of the nail. At this stage there is usually no pain, which is why many people miss it entirely. The spot sits close to the free edge, the part of the nail you trim, rather than near the cuticle.

Distal subungual onychomycosis is the most common form of nail fungus, beginning at the tip of the nail and spreading toward the cuticle. It accounts for the vast majority of cases people are describing when they search for early <strong>nail fungus symptoms</strong>. The nail itself stays mostly intact at this point. What changes is colour, not shape, and that single distinction is often the first clue separating early <strong>nail fungus symptoms</strong> from ordinary nail damage.

A second, less common early pattern looks completely different. White superficial onychomycosis affects only the nail surface, presenting as soft, powdery white patches that can be scraped away. Unlike the tip-first pattern, this version can appear anywhere on the nail plate and feels chalky rather than hard. Both patterns count as early <strong>nail fungus symptoms</strong>, even though they look almost nothing alike.

At this stage you may notice the nail looks slightly duller than the others, almost as if it has lost its shine. The surface might feel a touch rougher under a thumbnail run across it. Neither of these changes is dramatic. That is precisely the problem this guide exists to solve: early <strong>nail fungus symptoms</strong> are quiet, and quiet symptoms get postponed.

