## How Symptoms Change as the Infection Progresses

What looks like minor discolouration at this point has usually already progressed further than it appears on the surface. By the time a spot has grown noticeably or a second spot has joined it, <strong>nail fungus symptoms</strong> have moved past the nail's outer layer.

The colour spreads. Instead of a single contained patch, you will likely see a band or wedge shape stretching from the tip back toward the cuticle, often in yellow, brown, or a cloudy white. The edges of this discolouration are rarely sharp. They feather, blending into healthier nail rather than stopping cleanly, and this feathering is one of the more reliable mid-stage <strong>nail fungus symptoms</strong> to watch for.

Texture shifts alongside colour. The nail may start to feel thicker than its neighbours, and trimming it cleanly becomes harder. Some people describe it as the nail no longer cutting the way it used to, requiring more pressure or leaving a ragged edge instead of a clean line. This thickening is among the most commonly reported <strong>nail fungus symptoms</strong> at the progression stage.

This is also the stage where separation can begin. Onycholysis, the separation of the nail from the nail bed, signals that the infection has penetrated beyond the surface. You might notice the nail lifting slightly at the tip, with a small gap visible underneath where debris can collect. None of these <strong>nail fungus symptoms</strong> typically hurt yet, but they are no longer something to leave unchecked.

<hr className="my-8 border-gray-200" />

## Advanced Stage Symptoms: When Nail Fungus Becomes Serious

A nail that has reached the advanced stage tells you immediately, without needing a magnifying glass. The colour has deepened past yellow into brown, grey, or in some cases a greenish black, and the nail no longer resembles a healthy one in shape or texture.

Crumbling becomes the defining feature here. The nail edge breaks apart rather than cutting cleanly, sometimes shedding small flakes on its own. Underneath, a build-up of pale, chalky debris collects between the nail and the nail bed, a mix of fungal material and dead skin cells that thickens the nail further.

The nail's shape often distorts at this point too. It may curl, claw slightly at the edges, or simply look misshapen against the nail bed. On darker skin tones, the surrounding skin can take on a noticeably darker hue than its usual tone, while on lighter skin tones any redness around the nail fold tends to stand out more visibly.

Pain can appear here, though it is not guaranteed. When it does, it is usually from pressure: a thickened nail pressing against a shoe, or against another finger. A faint, unpleasant odour is common by this stage as well, especially when the nail is trimmed.

