## Symptoms That Mean You Need to See a Doctor

Pain, a nail that has turned brown or black rather than yellow, and any sign of spreading to a previously unaffected nail are the clearest signals that it is time to book a doctor's appointment rather than continue watching it. Diabetes or a weakened immune system lowers that threshold considerably.

A nail that suddenly hurts when it did not before usually means the infection has progressed enough to put pressure on the nail bed, or that a secondary bacterial infection has joined it. Redness, warmth, or swelling in the skin around the nail points in the same direction and should not wait.

Colour matters here too. Yellow and white patches sit at the lower end of concern. Brown or black discolouration, particularly if it appeared quickly rather than gradually, warrants a same-week appointment rather than a wait-and-watch approach, since it can occasionally signal something other than a routine fungal infection.

If you have diabetes, reduced circulation, or a condition affecting your immune system, even mild <strong>nail fungus symptoms</strong> are worth having checked sooner rather than later, because complications progress faster and recovery tends to take longer in these cases. At that point, your next step is to review <a href='/blog/nail-fungus/nail-fungus-treatment'>treatment options for nail fungus</a> with your doctor, since self-treating becomes considerably less reliable once an infection has reached this point.

<hr className="my-8 border-gray-200" />

## How to Know If Nail Fungus Is Spreading

Yes, nail fungus can spread to other nails, and in some cases to the surrounding skin. It moves through direct contact, so a fungal infection on one toe can transfer to the nail beside it, or to the skin between toes, causing athlete's foot.

The clearest sign of spreading is a new spot appearing on a nail that was previously clear, particularly one adjacent to the already infected nail. A second pattern to watch for is the original patch growing wider rather than simply staying the same size as the nail grows out.

Tracking this does not require anything technical. Photographing the affected nail every few weeks, in similar lighting, makes subtle growth far easier to catch than relying on memory. If the leading edge of the discolouration has visibly moved closer to the cuticle compared to a photo from a month earlier, that is movement worth acting on.

