## Reading Your Nails Instead of Guessing

A nail does not need to be painful to be worth paying attention to. That is the part most generic advice skips past entirely, and it is the part that matters most for anyone standing in front of a mirror trying to work out what they are actually looking at. The absence of pain is not reassurance. It is simply how this particular infection tends to behave early on.

What you do with that information is the real decision. A single faint spot near the tip, no spread, no pain, no diabetes in the picture, that is something to monitor and treat early. Brown discolouration, a second nail involved, or any pain changes the calculation immediately, and waiting stops being a reasonable choice. Trust what the nail is showing you over how minor it feels, and you will rarely get the timing wrong.
